14
00:01:55,323 --> 00:01:57,784
♪ You'd better stand
there's no turning back

15
00:01:57,992 --> 00:02:00,620
♪ The bugle sounds
the charge begins

16
00:02:00,829 --> 00:02:03,456
♪ But on this battlefield
no one wins

17
00:02:03,581 --> 00:02:06,417
♪ The smell of acrid smoke
and horse's breath

18
00:02:06,501 --> 00:02:08,795
♪ As I plunge on
into certain death

19
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:12,173
♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh
oh, oh, oh, oh

20
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,134
Hey, come on!

21
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,303
♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh
oh, oh, oh, oh

22
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:21,141
(GUITAR SOLO PLAYING)

23
00:02:40,702 --> 00:02:43,496
CROWD: (CHEERING)

24
00:02:50,753 --> 00:02:52,005
(SONG ENDS)

25
00:02:52,213 --> 00:02:53,214
Yeah!

26
00:02:53,381 --> 00:02:56,009
CROWD: (CHEERING)

27
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:05,643
It was always about the fans.

28
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:08,229
Always.

29
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:09,522
BRUCE: Thank you. Good night.

30
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:13,151
When you play in a country
for the first time

31
00:03:13,276 --> 00:03:15,445
or a city for the first time,

32
00:03:15,486 --> 00:03:18,323
you're going in there not
really knowing what to expect.

33
00:03:19,908 --> 00:03:23,328
But the intensity
of the reaction is amazing.

34
00:03:31,836 --> 00:03:33,880
Thank you! (LAUGHING)

35
00:03:35,673 --> 00:03:36,716
WOMAN: (SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

36
00:03:40,303 --> 00:03:42,388
(SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

37
00:03:44,807 --> 00:03:46,559
Er, thank you.

38
00:03:46,768 --> 00:03:48,394
The whole thing was
just like a dream.

39
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:54,234
It was like slow motion.

40
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:56,402
MAN: (LAUGHS)

41
00:03:58,071 --> 00:04:00,365
BRUCE: Surrounded by
hundreds of fans...

42
00:04:01,491 --> 00:04:02,408
twenty-four seven.

43
00:04:06,621 --> 00:04:07,413
CROWD: (CHEERING)

44
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,255
STEVE:
Any long careers,

45
00:04:15,421 --> 00:04:16,172
you're gonna have ups
and downs.

46
00:04:16,256 --> 00:04:17,715
There's no two ways about that.

47
00:04:17,799 --> 00:04:19,425
And you just kind of
ride with it.

48
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:22,470
But the hardcore fans

49
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:24,430
were just with you
through thick and thin.

50
00:04:26,099 --> 00:04:29,394
You know, it's quite amazing
to live and go through that.

51
00:04:33,064 --> 00:04:34,983
At the end of the day,

52
00:04:35,066 --> 00:04:37,068
I'd rather spend time with
the fans than anything else.

53
00:04:38,152 --> 00:04:40,363
CROWD 1 : Maiden! Maiden!

54
00:04:40,571 --> 00:04:42,448
CROWD 2 : Maiden! Maiden!

55
00:04:45,576 --> 00:04:47,203
MAN 1: It's about
a sense of belonging,

56
00:04:47,328 --> 00:04:49,580
belonging to
the Iron Maiden family.

57
00:04:51,124 --> 00:04:53,543
MAN 2: In that sort of
community, that bond,

58
00:04:53,626 --> 00:04:56,129
where we've connected on
a shared love

59
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:59,215
of the same music,
the same melodies,

60
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:02,385
they touch us in the same way
with different backgrounds.

61
00:05:02,510 --> 00:05:03,720
I do accounting work.

62
00:05:03,886 --> 00:05:04,971
I'm a psychiatrist.

63
00:05:05,054 --> 00:05:06,139
(IN SPANISH)

64
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:08,308
In the army.
I work in the bomb squad.

65
00:05:08,391 --> 00:05:09,684
I'm a musician.

66
00:05:09,809 --> 00:05:12,228
A lecturer in war studies
at King's College, London.

67
00:05:12,395 --> 00:05:13,980
Play in a heavy metal band.

68
00:05:14,105 --> 00:05:17,775
I'm a partner of the largest
Latin-American investment bank.

69
00:05:17,942 --> 00:05:19,986
I'm the CEO of
Metal Blade Records.

70
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:22,113
I'm a retired New York City
police sergeant.

71
00:05:22,238 --> 00:05:23,489
And I'm a music journalist.

72
00:05:25,825 --> 00:05:27,410
TOM MORELLO: It feels like
you're part of

73
00:05:27,577 --> 00:05:28,745
a exclusive club,

74
00:05:28,953 --> 00:05:31,039
but the only price of admission
is loving the band.

75
00:05:31,205 --> 00:05:33,499
Like, whenever I see a Maiden
shirt out in the wild,

76
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:34,834
you can kind of give 'em like
a bro nod

77
00:05:34,917 --> 00:05:36,878
and go like, "Right on,"
like, throw you the horns.

78
00:05:37,045 --> 00:05:38,379
TOM: (LAUGHS)

79
00:05:38,546 --> 00:05:40,214
CROWD: (CHANTING)

80
00:05:40,381 --> 00:05:42,008
JAVIER: Through all
the years, I've been going

81
00:05:42,133 --> 00:05:44,761
to hundreds and hundreds
of concerts.

82
00:05:44,886 --> 00:05:46,721
And I've been on the pit.
I've been on the first line.

83
00:05:46,804 --> 00:05:48,973
I've been there giving it all.

84
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:50,641
Now I'm older, I'm in the back.

85
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:53,311
But still, I jump and I scream.

86
00:05:53,394 --> 00:05:55,396
BRIAN SLAGEL:
You could feel this was legit.

87
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,232
Like, this was music being made
by people that loved this music

88
00:05:58,441 --> 00:06:01,444
and cared about this music
just as much as we did,

89
00:06:01,652 --> 00:06:03,529
even in those early days.

90
00:06:15,041 --> 00:06:16,918
STEVE: There's no way
I'd go on stage

91
00:06:17,043 --> 00:06:19,629
and play what I didn't
wanna play. You know?

92
00:06:19,796 --> 00:06:22,382
Even other... some... you know,
some other forms of rock music.

93
00:06:23,466 --> 00:06:24,759
I wouldn't... wouldn't do it.

94
00:06:24,884 --> 00:06:28,054
You know... I would rather
sweep the streets.

95
00:06:28,262 --> 00:06:30,598
- And I did in fact for a while.
- (CHUCKLES)

96
00:06:30,807 --> 00:06:34,060
I'm not interested in doing
sessions just for money.

97
00:06:34,143 --> 00:06:37,438
TOM: It's no secret that
Steve Harris is the one sort of

98
00:06:37,563 --> 00:06:41,109
steering the uncompromising
nature of Iron Maiden.

99
00:06:41,943 --> 00:06:44,195
When you say Steve Harris,
we just say The Boss.

100
00:06:44,362 --> 00:06:46,072
Because he is the mastermind.

101
00:06:46,948 --> 00:06:49,242
CHOP PITMAN: He was
an East Ender, just like us.

102
00:06:50,701 --> 00:06:53,329
He was a road sweeper.

103
00:06:53,496 --> 00:06:57,291
But he looked the coolest
road sweeper in all the world.

104
00:06:57,417 --> 00:07:00,253
He had the hair. He had the bags
coming out of his belt.

105
00:07:00,378 --> 00:07:03,673
I just knew that
he was gonna make it

106
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,260
because he had that way
about him, that determination.

107
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:10,263
LARS ULRICH: The Iron Maiden

108
00:07:10,304 --> 00:07:11,597
and the new wave
of British heavy metal

109
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:13,724
really was an answer

110
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:15,852
to everything that was happening

111
00:07:15,977 --> 00:07:17,520
at that time in England.

112
00:07:17,603 --> 00:07:18,729
NEWSREADER 1:
Are we going to have

113
00:07:18,896 --> 00:07:20,189
another winter of discontent

114
00:07:20,314 --> 00:07:22,066
as some politicians
have claimed?

115
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:23,401
NEWSREADER 2:
For many areas of Britain,

116
00:07:23,484 --> 00:07:25,319
chronic inflation
and unemployment

117
00:07:25,403 --> 00:07:26,612
was taking its toll.

118
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:28,739
STEVE: I mean,
we didn't have a lot.

119
00:07:29,699 --> 00:07:31,784
But then, no one did.

120
00:07:31,909 --> 00:07:33,786
You know, we didn't think,
"Oh, we ain't got anything."

121
00:07:33,953 --> 00:07:35,746
It was just the same
for everyone.

122
00:07:35,872 --> 00:07:37,457
So it was just pretty normal.

123
00:07:39,292 --> 00:07:40,501
At the time, all you've worried
about

124
00:07:40,668 --> 00:07:42,920
is getting a new amp
that sounds all right

125
00:07:43,004 --> 00:07:44,505
and some speakers
that ain't blown

126
00:07:44,630 --> 00:07:46,257
and you wanna just get out
and do some gigs.

127
00:07:49,051 --> 00:07:50,845
DAVE BEAZLEY: Steve said to me,
"We've got some gigs.

128
00:07:51,012 --> 00:07:52,680
"Would you like to do
a bit of roadie-ing?"

129
00:07:52,763 --> 00:07:53,848
And I said, "Yeah, of course."

130
00:07:54,015 --> 00:07:57,185
I said, "Oh, I'll make you
some lightboxes up."

131
00:07:57,226 --> 00:07:59,812
Back then, we used to break
into old warehouses

132
00:07:59,896 --> 00:08:03,357
and we'd get a big pram
and we'd load it all up,

133
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:04,901
anything I could get
my hands on

134
00:08:05,026 --> 00:08:07,987
and turn into lighting
of some description.

135
00:08:08,654 --> 00:08:10,656
And it just
progressed from there.

136
00:08:10,823 --> 00:08:12,492
STEVE: Even like
the first few gigs we did,

137
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:14,035
we were picking up fans
straight away,

138
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:15,244
and the reason being was

139
00:08:15,369 --> 00:08:16,913
'cause we were playing
our own material.

140
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:21,792
I mean, we were already
playing locally

141
00:08:22,001 --> 00:08:23,002
around the East End of London.

142
00:08:24,504 --> 00:08:27,590
But it started to
really snowball for us.

143
00:08:31,093 --> 00:08:33,846
CROWD: (CHANTING, CLAPPING)
Maiden! Maiden! Maiden!

144
00:08:34,055 --> 00:08:35,223
(THE IDES OF MARCH
BY IRON MAIDEN PLAYING)

145
00:08:35,389 --> 00:08:38,351
Maiden! Maiden!

146
00:08:42,605 --> 00:08:43,856
REPORTER:
Friday night at The Marquee,

147
00:08:44,023 --> 00:08:45,608
and East End rock band
Iron Maiden

148
00:08:45,775 --> 00:08:47,860
are playing the second of
three nights at the club.

149
00:08:47,902 --> 00:08:49,612
These gigs, at the end of
a gruelling

150
00:08:49,695 --> 00:08:51,864
concert hall tour
covering forty venues,

151
00:08:51,906 --> 00:08:55,535
are by way of a thank you
to their delirious fans.

152
00:08:55,618 --> 00:08:57,954
I mean, it's... So, like,
what is it, ten to eight

153
00:08:58,037 --> 00:08:59,413
and you can't get
in The Marquee?

154
00:08:59,455 --> 00:09:01,290
If I had a pound for every time
I've seen Iron Maiden

155
00:09:01,499 --> 00:09:02,583
I'd be a rich man by now.

156
00:09:03,334 --> 00:09:04,377
REPORTER: If the heavy metal
boom

157
00:09:04,585 --> 00:09:06,504
is being taken seriously
by the fans,

158
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:07,713
it's certainly causing a stir

159
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,633
in the offices of
the record companies.

160
00:09:10,841 --> 00:09:14,011
But when Iron Maiden formed,
punk was what was selling.

161
00:09:14,136 --> 00:09:16,138
And when the band were
first offered a recording deal

162
00:09:16,264 --> 00:09:18,724
they were told punk was
what they had to play.

163
00:09:18,766 --> 00:09:20,560
I didn't wanna play punk
or new wave.

164
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:23,521
I... I didn't wanna have
spiky hair

165
00:09:23,604 --> 00:09:25,648
and dress in, you know,
stupid clothes.

166
00:09:25,690 --> 00:09:26,732
You know what I mean?
(CHUCKLES)

167
00:09:26,816 --> 00:09:29,318
I wanted to play rock music.

168
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:32,405
STEVE: Labels tried to make us
more marketable, I suppose,

169
00:09:32,488 --> 00:09:33,698
'cause punk was the thing
that was happening,

170
00:09:33,739 --> 00:09:36,158
so they wanted to
take us down that road.

171
00:09:36,367 --> 00:09:38,995
Next thing would obviously be
for us to get our hair cut.

172
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:42,039
That would've been, "Fuck off."
(LAUGHS)

173
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:43,624
Luckily enough, Rod come along

174
00:09:43,791 --> 00:09:46,085
at a time
when it was just badly needed.

175
00:09:46,168 --> 00:09:48,713
And of course Andy
come along a few years later

176
00:09:48,796 --> 00:09:51,007
with all
the business side of stuff.

177
00:09:51,882 --> 00:09:53,301
They'd both go and bat away

178
00:09:53,426 --> 00:09:55,761
a lot of the issues that
we didn't wanna deal with.

179
00:09:55,803 --> 00:09:58,639
But Rod's the bombastic
Yorkshireman.

180
00:09:58,723 --> 00:10:02,435
He'll just take no prisoners
with the way he does things.

181
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:05,354
I put a barbed-wire fence
around the band creatively.

182
00:10:05,771 --> 00:10:08,274
No one gets within that fence.

183
00:10:08,441 --> 00:10:11,319
They do exactly
what they wanna do

184
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,739
with whoever they wanna
do it with and that's it.

185
00:10:14,822 --> 00:10:17,742
(PHANTOM OF THE OPERA
BY IRON MAIDEN PLAYING)

186
00:10:19,118 --> 00:10:21,203
PAUL:
♪ Keep your distance, walk away

187
00:10:21,370 --> 00:10:23,623
♪ Don't take his bait

188
00:10:23,706 --> 00:10:26,876
♪ Don't you stray

189
00:10:27,043 --> 00:10:28,753
♪ Don't fade away

190
00:10:29,378 --> 00:10:30,921
Yeah, come on!

191
00:10:32,590 --> 00:10:35,176
♪ Watch your step
He's out to get you

192
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:37,136
♪ Come what may

193
00:10:37,219 --> 00:10:40,222
♪ Don't you stray

194
00:10:40,264 --> 00:10:42,433
♪ From the narrow way

195
00:10:44,185 --> 00:10:47,229
We didn't get any radio play
back in the day.

196
00:10:47,271 --> 00:10:49,482
We didn't get any radio play
for many, many years actually.

197
00:10:49,607 --> 00:10:50,900
(GUITAR SOLO PLAYING)

198
00:10:51,025 --> 00:10:52,777
The industry didn't know
what to make of us.

199
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:54,111
They didn't know
what the appeal was.

200
00:10:55,154 --> 00:10:56,614
But it's just made everybody,

201
00:10:56,822 --> 00:10:58,991
the fans included,
just more tight

202
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:01,619
and, you know, just more
standing against it all.

203
00:11:02,912 --> 00:11:04,246
So we were a bit
of the underdog

204
00:11:04,455 --> 00:11:05,122
in the music scene,

205
00:11:06,123 --> 00:11:07,291
but we were the underdog

206
00:11:07,416 --> 00:11:08,751
at just
about everything really.

207
00:11:08,876 --> 00:11:09,919
(GUITAR SOLO CONTINUES)

208
00:11:17,218 --> 00:11:19,095
STEVE: I've never really liked
being in front of a camera,

209
00:11:19,261 --> 00:11:20,971
especially these days.

210
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,640
So we thought this time round

211
00:11:22,848 --> 00:11:24,433
it's much more interesting
to be

212
00:11:24,475 --> 00:11:27,144
looking at some other visuals
than us while we're talking.

213
00:11:27,269 --> 00:11:29,188
Because we're not
as good looking as Eddie.

214
00:11:29,230 --> 00:11:30,523
(LAUGHS)

215
00:11:32,149 --> 00:11:35,695
We weren't very familiar with
who these band members were.

216
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:37,321
We didn't know
any of their names

217
00:11:37,488 --> 00:11:39,365
because you had the album cover

218
00:11:39,448 --> 00:11:40,825
and there's Eddie.

219
00:11:42,076 --> 00:11:43,202
CHRIS DECHIARA:
He's face of the band.

220
00:11:44,620 --> 00:11:46,414
He's on every album cover.

221
00:11:47,289 --> 00:11:48,791
It's just one of those things
that draws you in,

222
00:11:48,999 --> 00:11:50,459
even without knowing the music.

223
00:11:53,504 --> 00:11:54,630
(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)

224
00:12:08,811 --> 00:12:10,980
WOMAN: I remember seeing Eddie
when I was a kid

225
00:12:11,105 --> 00:12:12,398
and just being like,
"What is that?"

226
00:12:13,899 --> 00:12:16,694
MAN: It kinda scared
the shit out of you.

227
00:12:16,777 --> 00:12:20,448
The origins of Eddie
really came from the fact that

228
00:12:20,614 --> 00:12:24,535
the guys in the band
were actually quite shy.

229
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:28,038
And I just dug the idea of
having a character

230
00:12:28,164 --> 00:12:31,167
to put across
the vibe of the band.

231
00:12:31,292 --> 00:12:34,003
So I was with EMI
having a meeting

232
00:12:34,170 --> 00:12:37,047
and I noticed some artwork on
the wall by Derek Riggs.

233
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:42,344
I had Derek come round
with samples of his artwork.

234
00:12:42,553 --> 00:12:44,764
In the middle of all that
was the first album sleeve.

235
00:12:44,847 --> 00:12:48,601
And we knew that
we'd found our guy.

236
00:12:48,684 --> 00:12:50,394
Well, Eddie can be
whatever you want him to be,

237
00:12:50,519 --> 00:12:52,229
that's the good thing about it
and that's one of the reasons

238
00:12:52,313 --> 00:12:53,814
why we've always had him
on the covers.

239
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:55,775
It means we ain't gotta be
on there for a start.

240
00:12:55,941 --> 00:12:58,360
MOLLY CAPOBIANCO:
Eddie is so cute.

241
00:12:58,569 --> 00:13:00,821
(GIGGLES) I love him.

242
00:13:00,946 --> 00:13:04,742
You know, he's the mascot
but he also is like the diva.

243
00:13:04,867 --> 00:13:07,119
You know, Eddie's the star.

244
00:13:07,286 --> 00:13:08,370
JAVIER BARDEM:
