1167
01:00:34,672 --> 01:00:37,550
BRUCE: Good night! Thank you!
Good night!

1168
01:00:37,634 --> 01:00:39,260
LARS: You know,
Maiden's rise was so quick

1169
01:00:39,385 --> 01:00:42,722
and so meteoric,
and I think that

1170
01:00:42,930 --> 01:00:45,683
when you're in your twenties
and into your early thirties,

1171
01:00:45,725 --> 01:00:47,435
you're so hungry

1172
01:00:47,602 --> 01:00:49,896
and you're so wanting-it-all

1173
01:00:49,937 --> 01:00:51,898
that you don't
even realise that

1174
01:00:52,106 --> 01:00:53,274
you actually have a say

1175
01:00:53,441 --> 01:00:56,486
and, "Wait a minute.
Like, we should take a break."

1176
01:00:58,154 --> 01:01:00,156
BRUCE: I... I looked
at the tour schedule

1177
01:01:00,615 --> 01:01:02,075
and there was one point

1178
01:01:02,200 --> 01:01:05,370
where we were doing
seven shows in a row,

1179
01:01:05,495 --> 01:01:06,954
then there was one day,

1180
01:01:07,038 --> 01:01:08,831
then there was six shows
in a row...

1181
01:01:08,956 --> 01:01:10,333
(CHUCKLING) ...and there
was one day,

1182
01:01:10,500 --> 01:01:12,502
and there was five shows
in a row.

1183
01:01:12,585 --> 01:01:14,921
Bruce, before you catch a cold,
just what's the...

1184
01:01:15,046 --> 01:01:16,005
I caught one already.

1185
01:01:16,130 --> 01:01:17,799
- It's too bloody late now.
- (LAUGHS)

1186
01:01:17,840 --> 01:01:18,800
- Er, what's...
- My manager.

1187
01:01:18,966 --> 01:01:21,052
Look, look, he's doing his job.

1188
01:01:21,177 --> 01:01:23,221
- I pay him 20% for this.
- Yes. I'm pretty sure...

1189
01:01:23,304 --> 01:01:24,764
BRUCE: I said to Rod,

1190
01:01:24,889 --> 01:01:27,975
"Erm, you realise that you're
dealing with human beings here?

1191
01:01:29,435 --> 01:01:32,355
"And that I'm the singer,

1192
01:01:32,522 --> 01:01:35,108
"and you can't
re-string a voice.

1193
01:01:35,316 --> 01:01:38,152
"So, if you want me to last
longer than five minutes,

1194
01:01:38,277 --> 01:01:41,823
"maybe we should consider
building in, like,

1195
01:01:42,824 --> 01:01:44,033
"a period of rest."

1196
01:01:45,326 --> 01:01:47,078
We were doing so many shows

1197
01:01:47,203 --> 01:01:50,873
that Nicko got blisters
all over his hands

1198
01:01:50,998 --> 01:01:52,041
and then they broke

1199
01:01:52,208 --> 01:01:54,001
and then he got blisters
under the blisters

1200
01:01:54,085 --> 01:01:56,546
and they broke
and then he got an infection.

1201
01:01:58,631 --> 01:02:01,926
There were plenty of casualties
from the '80s...

1202
01:02:01,968 --> 01:02:04,429
SECURITY GUARD: I want to see
the back of your robe.

1203
01:02:04,512 --> 01:02:06,389
BRUCE: ...you know, littering
the sidewalks of Los Angeles

1204
01:02:06,431 --> 01:02:07,807
and various other cities,
and I thought,

1205
01:02:07,890 --> 01:02:10,059
"I just do not want to be
one of those casualties."

1206
01:02:12,353 --> 01:02:13,229
No more!

1207
01:02:18,776 --> 01:02:20,903
We just did
the previous two albums

1208
01:02:21,028 --> 01:02:22,947
which was, er, "Somewhere
in Time" and "Seventh Son,"

1209
01:02:23,072 --> 01:02:26,659
which I was really proud of
production-wise, song-wise.

1210
01:02:26,784 --> 01:02:29,871
I thought they were really...
really a step on, you know?

1211
01:02:30,872 --> 01:02:31,914
And it seemed to me like

1212
01:02:32,039 --> 01:02:33,082
we were going backwards
doing it,

1213
01:02:33,249 --> 01:02:34,709
just going in
and bashing it out

1214
01:02:34,834 --> 01:02:37,545
in Steve's barn, you know?

1215
01:02:37,670 --> 01:02:41,215
We were getting ready to work
on "No Prayer for the Dying."

1216
01:02:41,299 --> 01:02:43,176
I desperately wanted it
to be a great album.

1217
01:02:44,802 --> 01:02:46,512
I had what they call
writer's block.

1218
01:02:46,596 --> 01:02:48,014
I desperately wanted to write,

1219
01:02:49,056 --> 01:02:51,017
I just didn't have anything.

1220
01:02:51,058 --> 01:02:54,520
And I think they sensed that
I... I wasn't happy with it.

1221
01:02:54,645 --> 01:02:58,149
It was time for a change.
For me and for them, really.

1222
01:02:58,232 --> 01:03:00,234
And so I left the band.

1223
01:03:01,986 --> 01:03:07,658
BRUCE: Adrian leaving was...
was a big thing for me.

1224
01:03:07,700 --> 01:03:10,536
And I didn't know what to do.
It's difficult to take it in.

1225
01:03:14,707 --> 01:03:17,126
STEVE: I just felt that
with Adrian, he wasn't happy

1226
01:03:17,168 --> 01:03:20,129
and the morale of the band
wasn't good.

1227
01:03:20,171 --> 01:03:21,130
You know, people have paid
their money

1228
01:03:21,255 --> 01:03:23,007
to come and see you,

1229
01:03:23,132 --> 01:03:26,344
you gotta give them a good
performance, the best you can.

1230
01:03:26,385 --> 01:03:28,262
JANICK GERS: I got a phone
call from, er, the boys

1231
01:03:28,429 --> 01:03:30,473
after Adrian Smith had left.

1232
01:03:30,556 --> 01:03:32,809
Erm, I think Adrian felt that
he wanted to continue

1233
01:03:32,892 --> 01:03:34,268
in a different direction.

1234
01:03:35,019 --> 01:03:37,104
(DRUMMING)

1235
01:03:40,566 --> 01:03:44,278
They asked me to go down there
and play these songs.

1236
01:03:44,362 --> 01:03:47,198
I wasn't quite aware
what was going on with Adrian.

1237
01:03:47,240 --> 01:03:48,282
Nobody told me.

1238
01:03:50,660 --> 01:03:52,703
I wasn't happy,
'cause his gear was there

1239
01:03:52,912 --> 01:03:55,164
and they wanted me to
play through his gear.

1240
01:03:55,373 --> 01:03:58,709
It felt wrong to me
that he wasn't there.

1241
01:03:58,876 --> 01:04:02,004
And in the end they just said,
"Listen, he's gone."

1242
01:04:02,171 --> 01:04:05,216
CAMERAMAN: In the dark,
Mr Janick Gers.

1243
01:04:05,258 --> 01:04:07,301
We've... We've locked him
into a very tight space,

1244
01:04:07,343 --> 01:04:09,345
so there'll be
no swinging around.

1245
01:04:09,387 --> 01:04:11,180
JANICK: No swinging,
no movement, no dancing.

1246
01:04:11,347 --> 01:04:12,515
JANICK: So we did a couple
of songs

1247
01:04:12,598 --> 01:04:13,724
and then we did "Trooper."

1248
01:04:13,891 --> 01:04:16,477
And it just...
I mean, I looked over at Dave

1249
01:04:16,602 --> 01:04:19,856
when we started playing.
It was just so powerful.

1250
01:04:19,897 --> 01:04:22,024
And when it finished
you were tingling.

1251
01:04:22,066 --> 01:04:24,402
The energy levels
were just beyond.

1252
01:04:24,485 --> 01:04:26,654
Oh, you are naughty,
but I love you.

1253
01:04:26,737 --> 01:04:28,197
JANICK: And then they asked me
to join.

1254
01:04:30,449 --> 01:04:32,410
INTERVIEWER: You've introduced
a new member to the band,

1255
01:04:32,577 --> 01:04:33,953
erm, Janick there.

1256
01:04:34,078 --> 01:04:35,955
Erm, how did he come
into the picture?

1257
01:04:37,498 --> 01:04:39,500
Well, basically, we just, erm...

1258
01:04:39,584 --> 01:04:42,003
(LAUGHING)

1259
01:04:42,128 --> 01:04:43,629
We just fucked him up.
We just...

1260
01:04:43,796 --> 01:04:45,590
After Adrian left the band
we just phoned him up and said,

1261
01:04:45,715 --> 01:04:47,466
"Look, can you learn four songs
by tomorrow?"

1262
01:04:47,508 --> 01:04:48,467
That phone box out there.

1263
01:04:48,509 --> 01:04:51,178
- And, er...
- (LAUGHS)

1264
01:04:51,304 --> 01:04:52,889
"Come down tomorrow
and try 'em out, you know?"

1265
01:04:52,972 --> 01:04:54,307
INTERVIEWER: How do you think
the new material's

1266
01:04:54,473 --> 01:04:55,975
gonna come across live?

1267
01:04:58,394 --> 01:05:01,147
It's gonna come across
amazingly, hopefully.

1268
01:05:01,188 --> 01:05:02,440
What do you think, Bruce?

1269
01:05:02,607 --> 01:05:03,858
(LAUGHING)

1270
01:05:04,025 --> 01:05:06,235
You, calm down! You, calm down!

1271
01:05:06,402 --> 01:05:08,029
No, it's gonna be amazing,
you know?

1272
01:05:08,195 --> 01:05:10,031
It's so... It's full of energy.

1273
01:05:10,114 --> 01:05:12,533
- Pass the buck, hey?
- Yeah, look here!

1274
01:05:12,700 --> 01:05:13,951
No, it's full of energy. It's...

1275
01:05:14,160 --> 01:05:15,953
We've just been rehearsing.
It's gonna be great.

1276
01:05:16,037 --> 01:05:17,204
BRUCE: No, it's gonna be mega.

1277
01:05:19,248 --> 01:05:21,292
(2 MINUTES TO MIDNIGHT
BY IRON MAIDEN PLAYING)

1278
01:05:21,417 --> 01:05:25,254
♪ The killer's breed
or the demon's seed

1279
01:05:25,379 --> 01:05:29,216
♪ The glamour, the fortune
the pain

1280
01:05:30,176 --> 01:05:31,886
Halfway through the next tour

1281
01:05:31,969 --> 01:05:34,764
Bruce told me, "I'm leaving
the band, all right?"

1282
01:05:34,805 --> 01:05:38,434
And we still had, like,
two months of touring to do.

1283
01:05:38,517 --> 01:05:43,064
So I'm sitting on stage behind
him every fucking night

1284
01:05:43,189 --> 01:05:44,357
looking at his sorry arse,

1285
01:05:44,440 --> 01:05:46,567
knowing he don't wanna be
in Iron Maiden.

1286
01:05:52,239 --> 01:05:57,662
I took it as an affront to
the fans that he was up there

1287
01:05:57,828 --> 01:05:59,914
knowing full well
he didn't wanna be there.

1288
01:06:00,039 --> 01:06:02,375
No, I was really, really
fucking angry at him.

1289
01:06:05,836 --> 01:06:09,465
ROSS: They were playing
Wembley Arena

1290
01:06:09,507 --> 01:06:11,842
and Bruce was singing like...
(INAUDIBLE)

1291
01:06:11,926 --> 01:06:13,719
...and nothing was coming out.

1292
01:06:13,844 --> 01:06:15,137
And I remember Harris

1293
01:06:15,262 --> 01:06:17,932
looking at the monitor guy
and the sound guy, going...

1294
01:06:18,057 --> 01:06:19,642
(INAUDIBLE)

1295
01:06:19,684 --> 01:06:21,978
You know? And there's nothing
coming out.

1296
01:06:22,103 --> 01:06:24,063
And then it slowly dawned
on Steve

1297
01:06:24,188 --> 01:06:26,440
that Bruce wasn't singing.

1298
01:06:26,565 --> 01:06:28,985
And that's when I realised
things were not good.

1299
01:06:29,026 --> 01:06:30,486
CROWD: (CHEERING)

1300
01:06:30,528 --> 01:06:34,824
Thank you! Good night
from Iron Maiden!

1301
01:06:34,949 --> 01:06:37,952
It has been
a very good ten years.

1302
01:06:39,078 --> 01:06:41,998
We will all see you soon...

1303
01:06:43,040 --> 01:06:44,125
I hope.

1304
01:06:45,918 --> 01:06:46,919
Good night.

1305
01:06:48,671 --> 01:06:50,047
TOM: When you're in that,
kind of,

1306
01:06:50,131 --> 01:06:52,591
chosen family of a band,

1307
01:06:52,717 --> 01:06:54,385
it's often brutal.

1308
01:06:54,552 --> 01:06:56,887
Sometimes it's difficult
and the pieces don't fit.

1309
01:06:56,929 --> 01:07:00,891
And for your own sanity you need
to be away from it.

1310
01:07:00,975 --> 01:07:04,186
- (BANGING)
- (INDISTINCT SHOUTING)

1311
01:07:04,228 --> 01:07:06,772
(DISTANT LAUGHTER)

1312
01:07:06,897 --> 01:07:10,860
(INDISTINCT VOICE ON PA)

1313
01:07:14,697 --> 01:07:18,659
I thought of packing it in...
completely.

1314
01:07:18,784 --> 01:07:20,995
I thought, "Is all this
worth it, this madness?"

1315
01:07:22,747 --> 01:07:26,917
I was ready for
the funny farm... genuinely.

1316
01:07:27,084 --> 01:07:29,336
(DIE WITH YOUR BOOTS ON
BY IRON MAIDEN PLAYING)

1317
01:07:49,065 --> 01:07:50,066
BRUCE: ♪ Die!

1318
01:07:52,401 --> 01:07:53,319
NEWSREADER:
Iron Maiden couldn't be

1319
01:07:53,402 --> 01:07:54,695
at Donington this year.

1320
01:07:54,862 --> 01:07:57,448
Their current world tour
finishes in two weeks' time

1321
01:07:57,490 --> 01:08:00,034
and then the search is on
for a new lead singer.

1322
01:08:00,117 --> 01:08:01,452
Bruce Dickinson is leaving
the group

1323
01:08:01,494 --> 01:08:03,412
to pursue his solo career.

1324
01:08:03,454 --> 01:08:04,872
So, Bruce, why are you leaving
Iron Maiden?

1325
01:08:04,955 --> 01:08:07,917
Erm, in a short...

1326
01:08:07,958 --> 01:08:10,252
A short answer,
to do something different.

1327
01:08:10,377 --> 01:08:11,212
INTERVIEWER:
And what would that be?

1328
01:08:11,337 --> 01:08:13,631
Erm...
a different kind of music.

1329
01:08:13,756 --> 01:08:15,341
(SHOOT ALL THE CLOWNS
BY BRUCE DICKINSON PLAYING)

1330
01:08:15,466 --> 01:08:18,803
♪ I've been down
at the crazy house

1331
01:08:19,011 --> 01:08:22,807
♪ I've been playing
with the cat and the mouse

1332
01:08:23,015 --> 01:08:24,183
The bombshell hit

1333
01:08:24,266 --> 01:08:26,602
when Bruce decided
to fuck off from Maiden.

1334
01:08:29,105 --> 01:08:32,233
If he doesn't wanna be
here then, "See you later."

1335
01:08:34,985 --> 01:08:36,695
JAVIER: When I heard
Bruce was leaving I was like,

1336
01:08:36,904 --> 01:08:38,239
"No, he cannot leave."

1337
01:08:38,447 --> 01:08:41,408
It was definitely, for me,
shocking.

1338
01:08:41,450 --> 01:08:43,285
It's not something
you wanna hear

1339
01:08:43,410 --> 01:08:45,621
about your...
your favourite band.

1340
01:08:45,788 --> 01:08:47,832
He'd already left
when I found out.

1341
01:08:47,915 --> 01:08:49,250
And I was kinda like,
"Oh my God,

1342
01:08:49,416 --> 01:08:50,584
"what the fuck just happened?"

1343
01:08:52,419 --> 01:08:54,380
NAVID RASHID: We sort of hold
our heroes to this standard

1344
01:08:54,505 --> 01:08:56,841
that they just have to continue
to be what we want them to be

1345
01:08:56,966 --> 01:08:58,342
and what we expect them to be,

1346
01:08:58,509 --> 01:09:00,469
but that doesn't really allow
for an artist to grow,

1347
01:09:00,511 --> 01:09:02,054
and he is, you know,
a consummate artist

1348
01:09:02,179 --> 01:09:03,597
and I'm sure he needed it.

1349
01:09:04,807 --> 01:09:07,101
So, Steve, what are you gonna do
for a singer?

1350
01:09:07,184 --> 01:09:08,477
You know, we've been listening
to tapes.

1351
01:09:08,519 --> 01:09:09,812
We've got, like, about...
I don't know.

1352
01:09:09,937 --> 01:09:13,649
about five suitcases, er,
full of tapes to listen to.

1353
01:09:14,024 --> 01:09:16,402
And, er, you know,

1354
01:09:16,443 --> 01:09:19,822
hopefully we'll find somebody
who's, er, reasonably unknown,

1355
01:09:19,947 --> 01:09:22,491
but maybe played
on a club circuit somewhere,

1356
01:09:22,658 --> 01:09:24,618
had a bit of experience
with audiences.

1357
01:09:24,702 --> 01:09:27,830
Erm, but I think it will be
exciting to find somebody

1358
01:09:27,955 --> 01:09:32,668
who hasn't really, you know,
made it or whatever, you know?

1359
01:09:32,751 --> 01:09:34,795
Now this lot haven't been on
since the year '81.

1360
01:09:34,920 --> 01:09:37,214
They're good heavy rockers,
just here to have fun.

1361
01:09:37,381 --> 01:09:40,759
They're called Iron Maiden,
with new man Blaze Bayley.

1362
01:09:40,885 --> 01:09:44,263
♪ The freeway is jammed
and it's backed up for miles

1363
01:09:44,346 --> 01:09:48,017
♪ The car is an oven
and baking us wild

1364
01:09:48,058 --> 01:09:51,645
♪ Nothing is ever
the way it should be

1365
01:09:51,770 --> 01:09:55,107
♪ What we deserve
we just don't get, you see

1366
01:09:55,316 --> 01:09:56,442
RORY MCGUCKIN: Blaze coming in,

1367
01:09:56,483 --> 01:09:57,943
he brought a different sound
to Maiden.

1368
01:09:58,068 --> 01:09:59,320
And I love his voice.

1369
01:09:59,445 --> 01:10:02,406
And, like, Blaze is a very
accomplished metal singer.

1370
01:10:02,489 --> 01:10:05,159
JAMES TUCKER: I'm almost
shocked that he took the job.

1371
01:10:06,660 --> 01:10:08,204
I'm sure it was amazing
to hear,

1372
01:10:08,370 --> 01:10:09,538
"Yeah, we want you for
the band."

1373
01:10:09,663 --> 01:10:12,041
I'm sure he was partying
that night.

1374
01:10:12,166 --> 01:10:13,959
But I'm sure when he woke up
the next day he was thinking,

1375
01:10:14,084 --> 01:10:15,377
"Oh shit."

1376
01:10:16,337 --> 01:10:18,005
Steve Harris said to me,

1377
01:10:18,172 --> 01:10:20,382
"What we do, we do together.

1378
01:10:20,507 --> 01:10:24,094
"And I don't care who writes
the music,

1379
01:10:24,178 --> 01:10:25,262
"who writes the songs,

1380
01:10:25,346 --> 01:10:27,640
"as long as it's great."

1381
01:10:27,723 --> 01:10:30,976
But still, it's a massive job.

1382
01:10:31,101 --> 01:10:35,314
I was very surprised
that they selected me.

1383
01:10:37,358 --> 01:10:38,484
I've met a lot of fans

1384
01:10:38,609 --> 01:10:41,987
and, er, generally the...
the reaction has been,

1385
01:10:42,112 --> 01:10:44,323
to their credit, they've just
said, "Well, good luck."

1386
01:10:44,531 --> 01:10:47,451
You know? And I think they just
wanna see the band do well.

1387
01:10:47,493 --> 01:10:49,954
And, erm, I'm looking forward
to going on tour.

1388
01:10:50,037 --> 01:10:53,749
But the... the main thing is,
I can't replace Bruce Dickinson.

1389
01:10:54,750 --> 01:10:55,793
DR PURI: Blaze Bayley,

1390
01:10:55,960 --> 01:10:58,087
he was an up-and-coming
British vocalist,

1391
01:10:58,212 --> 01:11:01,131
but he didn't have the range
that Bruce has.

1392
01:11:01,715 --> 01:11:04,468
SCOTT: It was such a hard spot,

1393
01:11:04,551 --> 01:11:06,220
I think, for that guy.

1394
01:11:06,387 --> 01:11:08,180
And he sounded so different too.

1395
01:11:08,222 --> 01:11:11,392
It wasn't like they got a guy
who could at least sing what...

1396
01:11:11,517 --> 01:11:13,602
the way Bruce sang
on those songs.

1397
01:11:13,769 --> 01:11:16,438
♪ Roll of the dice
Take a spin of the wheel

1398
01:11:16,605 --> 01:11:18,274
NAVID:
Many of us sort of wonder why

1399
01:11:18,399 --> 01:11:22,903
they didn't do more to work
with his natural ability.

1400
01:11:23,070 --> 01:11:25,364
But, you know,
he brought his own spin

1401
01:11:25,489 --> 01:11:27,449
to the Iron Maiden sound.

1402
01:11:27,616 --> 01:11:29,201
(THE TROOPER
BY IRON MAIDEN PLAYING)

1403
01:11:32,830 --> 01:11:34,873
DOM: I thought Blaze did
an absolutely brilliant job,

1404
01:11:34,999 --> 01:11:35,916
particularly live.

1405
01:11:36,041 --> 01:11:37,793
You know, I think he was...

1406
01:11:37,918 --> 01:11:39,461
You know, I don't think
you could fault him

1407
01:11:39,586 --> 01:11:40,796
for his contribution
to Iron Maiden.

1408
01:11:42,548 --> 01:11:44,842
Some fans were screaming,
"Fuck Blaze,"

1409
01:11:44,967 --> 01:11:47,636
during the shows
and I think that's quite unfair.

1410
01:11:49,888 --> 01:11:51,098
TOMINA FLORENCIA RAMAZZOTTI:
(IN SPANISH)

1411
01:11:59,690 --> 01:12:01,692
BLAZE: (IN ENGLISH)
I fucking told you!

1412
01:12:01,817 --> 01:12:05,988
I fucking told ya!
Right, I fucking told ya!

1413
01:12:06,030 --> 01:12:09,033
Can you see? Can you see
who it fucking is?

1414
01:12:09,116 --> 01:12:10,868
Get it! Fucking get it!

1415
01:12:10,951 --> 01:12:12,703
DOM: There was a time
in the early days of punk

1416
01:12:12,828 --> 01:12:16,040
in the late '70s when,
er, spitting was something

1417
01:12:16,206 --> 01:12:19,376
that fans did
to show their appreciation.

1418
01:12:19,460 --> 01:12:22,379
That never sort of transferred
to the metal scene at all.

1419
01:12:22,588 --> 01:12:26,258
You know, spitting at
a metal show is insane.

1420
01:12:26,342 --> 01:12:29,386
Any human being would object
thoroughly to being spat on.

1421
01:12:29,428 --> 01:12:30,679
Fucking had it!

1422
01:12:31,597 --> 01:12:33,015
TOMINA: (IN SPANISH)

1423
01:12:45,486 --> 01:12:47,738
The Blaze era of Maiden,

1424
01:12:47,821 --> 01:12:49,531
- there was great moments.
- BLAZE: I'll fucking kill you!

1425
01:12:49,698 --> 01:12:51,325
NICKO: But there were many
that weren't.

1426
01:12:53,202 --> 01:12:56,288
DOM: I don't think
any of the commercial decline

1427
01:12:56,455 --> 01:12:58,791
was Blaze's fault.

1428
01:13:01,794 --> 01:13:04,338
That was just circumstance.

1429
01:13:04,505 --> 01:13:06,507
- Anyone else?
- BLAZE: The mainstream

1430
01:13:06,590 --> 01:13:10,719
heavy metal rock press
were saying,

1431
01:13:10,844 --> 01:13:12,137
- "Maiden are dead."
- BLAZE: Fuckin' had it!

1432
01:13:12,304 --> 01:13:14,473
"Maiden are over.

1433
01:13:15,641 --> 01:13:17,101
"We have Nirvana.

1434
01:13:17,226 --> 01:13:19,269
"We have grunge.

1435
01:13:19,353 --> 01:13:21,480
"That is the future.

1436
01:13:21,563 --> 01:13:23,565
"That is what's coming."

1437
01:13:23,690 --> 01:13:26,985
Everyone that wasn't grunge,
more or less,

1438
01:13:27,194 --> 01:13:28,362
were considered like...

1439
01:13:28,487 --> 01:13:32,699
like a old reptile or whatever,
you know?

1440
01:13:32,866 --> 01:13:34,576
They... They just looked at you

1441
01:13:34,743 --> 01:13:36,578
like you were
an idiot or something.

1442
01:13:36,703 --> 01:13:38,497
Heavy metal
wasn't cool any more.

1443
01:13:38,622 --> 01:13:40,874
The '90s was crap.

1444
01:13:40,999 --> 01:13:42,960
Everything was just like...

1445
01:13:43,085 --> 01:13:46,422
too big clothes and ugly shit.
(CHUCKLING) You know?

1446
01:13:47,714 --> 01:13:49,758
The grungy scene was happening.

1447
01:13:49,925 --> 01:13:52,261
I think the Seattle bands were
coming in.

1448
01:13:52,428 --> 01:13:53,720
I think, actually,
we asked Nirvana

1449
01:13:53,846 --> 01:13:54,972
if they want to support us

1450
01:13:55,139 --> 01:13:56,598
and they said, "No,
we're too old for them."

1451
01:13:56,723 --> 01:13:58,392
(LAUGHS)

1452
01:13:59,893 --> 01:14:01,395
ROB: During the Blaze years,

1453
01:14:01,478 --> 01:14:04,731
I saw them go towards
small clubs,

1454
01:14:05,566 --> 01:14:08,360
probably 2,000-seat venues,

1455
01:14:08,485 --> 01:14:09,820
and it was just...

1456
01:14:09,945 --> 01:14:12,990
It was intimate,
is how I would describe it.

1457
01:14:13,031 --> 01:14:15,993
I saw them on The X Factor tour,
erm, in Philadelphia

1458
01:14:16,118 --> 01:14:18,662
at this small place called
the Electric Factory.

1459
01:14:18,829 --> 01:14:21,748
I think it fits a thousand,
1,500 people.

1460
01:14:21,832 --> 01:14:24,501
There weren't even
500 in the arena.

1461
01:14:24,585 --> 01:14:26,795
Now, instead of playing

1462
01:14:27,004 --> 01:14:30,132
in, you know, arenas
and big venues,

1463
01:14:30,257 --> 01:14:34,011
we were back doing clubs again.

1464
01:14:34,136 --> 01:14:36,847
That kind of really put
a few chinks in the armour

1465
01:14:36,930 --> 01:14:38,390
of the band as a whole.

1466
01:14:39,892 --> 01:14:42,436
Yeah, we had... we had a bit of
a rough time in America.

1467
01:14:42,519 --> 01:14:45,272
The fans seemed
to desert us there.

1468
01:14:45,397 --> 01:14:48,233
Er, but the rest of the world,
we... we still did fine.

1469
01:14:48,358 --> 01:14:51,153
Not the same, admittedly,
but we did okay.

1470
01:14:52,988 --> 01:14:55,699
I think Steve had been
beaten up

1471
01:14:55,782 --> 01:14:57,409
mentally, emotionally,

1472
01:14:57,451 --> 01:15:01,455
by a bruising few years

1473
01:15:01,622 --> 01:15:03,373
when it hadn't quite worked out

1474
01:15:03,499 --> 01:15:05,167
the way he thought it was
gonna work out.

1475
01:15:07,085 --> 01:15:09,046
It was a challenging time
'cause Bruce left

1476
01:15:09,171 --> 01:15:10,464
and then I went through
a divorce as well,

1477
01:15:10,547 --> 01:15:13,217
so there was all that going on
as well.

1478
01:15:13,383 --> 01:15:15,469
But I'm really proud of
that period, really,

1479
01:15:15,552 --> 01:15:17,387
because we were just
up against it.

1480
01:15:17,429 --> 01:15:20,057
We were fighting, you know,
and I like that.

1481
01:15:20,140 --> 01:15:21,725
That's a challenge.

1482
01:15:21,767 --> 01:15:23,352
And all the hardcore fans
were still there.

1483
01:15:24,770 --> 01:15:27,314
RORY: It's important to
really understand the Blaze era

1484
01:15:27,439 --> 01:15:29,483
and respect the Blaze era
and him

1485
01:15:29,650 --> 01:15:30,817
and what he brought to the band.

1486
01:15:30,901 --> 01:15:33,820
And I think the two albums
are fantastic.

1487
01:15:33,904 --> 01:15:36,156
They're so dark.
They're so different.

1488
01:15:36,657 --> 01:15:38,075
But they're great albums.

1489
01:15:38,116 --> 01:15:41,453
We all have, er, er,
a lot of respect for Blaze.

1490
01:15:41,620 --> 01:15:45,874
But I guess I'm not gonna
surprise anybody if I say that,

1491
01:15:45,999 --> 01:15:48,544
you know, Bruce is the voice
of Iron Maiden.

1492
01:15:48,669 --> 01:15:50,504
And, you know,
that's what we grew up with.

1493
01:15:50,671 --> 01:15:51,672
That's what we want. (CHUCKLES)

1494
01:15:53,006 --> 01:15:55,300
I remember saying to Steve,
you know,

1495
01:15:55,425 --> 01:15:58,470
I heard Bruce would like to
come back into the band again,

1496
01:15:58,595 --> 01:16:01,473
and he was, like, a little bit
shocked, I think, you know?

1497
01:16:01,682 --> 01:16:03,559
And, and I was trying to
convince him that it might...

1498
01:16:03,684 --> 01:16:05,143
this is a good thing to do.

1499
01:16:07,187 --> 01:16:10,065
I think we accept we weren't
a world-class band any more.

1500
01:16:10,190 --> 01:16:13,485
Like Maiden,
Bruce is struggling a bit.

1501
01:16:13,652 --> 01:16:15,946
His solo project didn't go
quite the way he expected

1502
01:16:16,113 --> 01:16:17,948
and I think he... he...
he really missed it.

1503
01:16:18,115 --> 01:16:20,325
You know, Maiden's just got
a special vibe.

1504
01:16:20,367 --> 01:16:21,577
They belong together.

1505
01:16:26,456 --> 01:16:29,876
When I got the news,
it was horrible, it was awful.

1506
01:16:29,960 --> 01:16:33,797
I absolutely loved being
in Iron Maiden.

1507
01:16:33,964 --> 01:16:37,342
But it doesn't matter
if I'm there or not.

1508
01:16:37,509 --> 01:16:43,098
Planet Earth is a better place
for having Iron Maiden in it.

1509
01:16:47,019 --> 01:16:49,062
That was when I wanted
to get Adrian back in.

1510
01:16:49,229 --> 01:16:50,397
Getting the band back together.

1511
01:16:52,107 --> 01:16:54,151
Everybody was a little bit
more grown up

1512
01:16:54,276 --> 01:16:56,069
and everyone was a little bit
more open.

1513
01:16:57,404 --> 01:16:59,031
And it was... it was great.

1514
01:16:59,156 --> 01:17:01,783
I sort of enjoyed the second
time round a bit more.

1515
01:17:01,825 --> 01:17:02,951
You know,
I was much more confident.

1516
01:17:04,119 --> 01:17:05,787
To me it was like a, you know,

1517
01:17:05,829 --> 01:17:07,956
second bite at the cherry,
you know?

1518
01:17:08,081 --> 01:17:09,458
All the things that
I maybe regretted

1519
01:17:09,666 --> 01:17:11,126
or thought I could've
done better

